@page "/todos"
@using Jewel.JPMS.Contracts.Todos
@using Jewel.JPMS.Features.Todos
@inject AuthService Auth
@inject SessionService Session
@inject ITodoStore TodoStore
@inject NavigationManager Nav
@inject Jewel.JPMS.Features.Projects.ProjectListReadModel Projects
@inject TodoViewStorage ViewStorage

<PageTitle>To-dos · JPMS</PageTitle>

@*
    The master to-do list — revived from nav retirement as the Internal folder's Todo entry
    (decision 2026-07-22: to-dos are always internal work, sometimes project-specific, so there
    are TWO ways to see them: this page is every to-do — company-wide and project — with a project
    filter; the project folder keeps its own To-do tab scoped to one project).

    The managing director and administrators see EVERY item — general (company-wide, no-project)
    items and every project's items — and can add anything, tick anything off, or delete.
    Everyone else sees only the items assigned to a ROLE THEY HOLD (general and project alike,
    read through ListMyTodoItems), minus any pinned to a different named person, and can tick
    those off. Items are assigned to a role first — so they survive staff changes — optionally
    pinned to a named holder, which keeps the item to that one person's list. General items are
    captured from a company-wide email at triage ("General to-do") or added here directly.

    ADDING is project-aware, and wider than reading (decision 2026-07-31). The add modal leads with
    a Project picker defaulted from the scope filter — raising an item while Ravenswood is picked
    makes a Ravenswood item, which is exactly what a button labelled "Add general to-do" used to get
    wrong. Blank means company-wide, and only the managing director / administrators may leave it
    blank (the api's AddGeneralTodoItem gate). Everyone else in the manage gate — project managers,
    site managers, the FD, accounts — gets the button for PROJECT items, mirroring AddTodoItem's own
    gate and the project To-do tab, where those people have always been able to add. Their list here
    is still only their own items, so an item they add for another role is confirmed by reference
    with a link to the project's tab rather than silently vanishing.

    Each row is a fixed-height line of title + key facts (never the multiline notes); clicking one
    opens the item's own page (/todos/{id}) with the full detail including those notes, the
    to-dos linked to it, its communications — answerable there — and the complete/reopen,
    reassign, move and delete actions. The board's drag-to-complete stays here.
*@

@if (!sessionReady)
{
    <section class="px-6 md:px-8 py-12">
        <LoadGate IsLoading="true" Prominent="true" Label="Loading" />
    </section>
}
else if (!Session.IsApproved)
{
    <RequestAccessView Email="@Auth.CurrentUser!.Email" DisplayName="@Auth.CurrentUser.DisplayName" />
}
else if (!HasInternalRole)
{
    <section class="px-6 md:px-8 py-12">
        <h1 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-content mb-2">Not available</h1>
        <p class="text-content-muted">The to-do list is an internal Jewel surface.</p>
    </section>
}
else
{
    <section class="px-6 py-6 md:px-8">

        <header class="mb-6 flex flex-wrap items-end justify-between gap-3">
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow mb-1">JPMS</p>
                <h1 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-semibold text-content leading-tight">To-dos</h1>
                <p class="text-sm text-content-muted mt-1">
                    @(CanSeeAll
                        ? "The master list: every to-do item — company-wide and project items alike. Each project's To-do tab shows just its own."
                        : "The items assigned to your roles or to you by name — company-wide and project items alike.")
                </p>
            </div>
            @* One button, not two: the project picker inside decides whether the item lands on a
               project or company-wide, so "Add to-do" is honest for both. Gated on CanManage, the
               wider add gate — a PM raising a project item is the common case. *@
            @if (CanManage)
            {
                <button type="button" class="btn-primary text-xs px-3 py-2" @onclick="OpenAdd">Add to-do</button>
            }
        </header>

        @if (error is not null)
        {
            <Notice Tone="Tone.Negative" Class="mb-3">@error</Notice>
        }

        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 mb-4">
            @* Keyword search, the project tab's idiom brought to the master list: filters the
               to-dos (reference, title, notes, pinned assignee — "todo-0011" finds the item) and
               drives the tagged-email lookup at the foot of the panel. A query looks across EVERY
               status — the whole point of searching is finding the thing wherever it is filed —
               so the status tabs stand down while one is typed. *@
            <div class="relative flex-1 min-w-[220px]">
                <input type="search" class="field w-full text-sm pr-8" placeholder="Search to-dos — reference (TODO-0011), title or notes"
                       value="@search" @oninput="OnSearchInput" />
                @if (HasQuery)
                {
                    <button type="button" title="Clear search"
                            class="absolute right-2 top-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 text-content-faint hover:text-content-muted text-sm leading-none"
                            @onclick="ClearSearch">✕</button>
                }
            </div>

            @* Board or list — one preference shared by every to-do surface (TodoViewStorage). The
               board lays Open and Done side by side, so the status tabs are the LIST's own control
               and step aside while the board is up rather than sitting there answering nothing. *@
            <div class="flex items-center gap-1 mr-1">
                <button type="button" class="@ViewTabClass(true)" title="Status board — drag a card between columns to change its status" @onclick="() => SetView(true)">Board</button>
                <button type="button" class="@ViewTabClass(false)" title="Flat list with status tabs" @onclick="() => SetView(false)">List</button>
            </div>

            @if (!boardView)
            {
                @* Disabled (never hidden) while a query is live — the search looks across every
                   status, and a tab that quietly did nothing would read as a broken filter. *@
                <button type="button" class="@StatusTabClass(StatusFilter.Open) disabled:opacity-60" disabled="@HasQuery"
                        title="@(HasQuery ? "Search looks across every status — clear it to use the tabs" : null)"
                        @onclick="() => statusFilter = StatusFilter.Open">Open</button>
                <button type="button" class="@StatusTabClass(StatusFilter.Done) disabled:opacity-60" disabled="@HasQuery"
                        title="@(HasQuery ? "Search looks across every status — clear it to use the tabs" : null)"
                        @onclick="() => statusFilter = StatusFilter.Done">Done</button>
                <button type="button" class="@StatusTabClass(StatusFilter.All) disabled:opacity-60" disabled="@HasQuery"
                        title="@(HasQuery ? "Search looks across every status — clear it to use the tabs" : null)"
                        @onclick="() => statusFilter = StatusFilter.All">All</button>
            }

            @* The selects are built from data — the scope filter from the projects that have
               items, the role and person filters from the assignable pool — so they wait with the
               list. A short list of options that quietly grows misleads exactly as a 0 count does. *@
            @* The project filter: every project with items, plus company-wide (no-project) only.
               Disabled until the options land — a short list that quietly grows misleads as a 0
               count does — but a filter is a control, so it says so in its own placeholder. *@
            <select class="field !w-auto text-xs" value="@scopeFilter" @onchange="e => scopeFilter = e.Value?.ToString() ?? ScopeAll"
                    disabled="@(!filtersReady)">
                <option value="@ScopeAll">@(filtersReady ? "All" : "Loading…")</option>
                <option value="@ScopeGeneral">Company-wide</option>
                @foreach (var project in ProjectsWithItems)
                {
                    <option value="@project.ProjectId">@project.Reference — @project.Name</option>
                }
            </select>

            @* The role filter is EVERYONE's (a reader holding several roles narrows to one of
               them); its options are the roles that actually have items on this list — built from
               data like the scope filter, so it needs no assignable-pool fetch and never offers a
               row that matches nothing. Unassigned is a role-slot answer, so it lives here — and
               empties the person filter. *@
            <select class="field !w-auto text-xs" value="@roleFilter" @onchange="OnRoleFilterChange"
                    disabled="@(!filtersReady)">
                <option value="">@(filtersReady ? "Any role" : "Loading…")</option>
                @if (CanSeeAll || HasUnassigned)
                {
                    <option value="@Unassigned">Unassigned</option>
                }
                @foreach (var role in RolesOnList)
                {
                    <option value="@((int)role)">@role.DisplayName()</option>
                }
            </select>

            @if (CanSeeAll)
            {
                @* Disabled until the pool lands, never absent: a filter that pops into the row a
                   second late is a control the reader has already decided isn't there. Role and
                   person are two filters, not one combined list: pick a role to narrow the person
                   list to its holders, a person straight away to see everything pinned to them,
                   or both to intersect. *@
                <select class="field !w-auto text-xs" value="@personFilter" @onchange="e => personFilter = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty"
                        disabled="@(!filtersReady || roleFilter == Unassigned)">
                    <option value="">@(filtersReady ? "Anyone" : "Loading…")</option>
                    @foreach (var person in FilterablePeople)
                    {
                        <option value="@person.Email">@person.DisplayName</option>
                    }
                </select>
            }

            @* Narrowed to one project, the reader is really asking for that project's To-do tab —
               where the list is only its items and nothing needs filtering. A line of text, so it
               simply doesn't render until the project labels land. *@
            @if (ScopedProject is Project scoped)
            {
                <a class="text-xs text-content-muted underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-content"
                   href="@($"/projects/{scoped.ProjectId}/todos")">
                    Open @(scoped.Name)'s To-do tab
                </a>
            }
        </div>

        @if (addedNote is not null)
        {
            <p class="rounded-lg bg-surface-raised border border-line px-3 py-2 text-sm text-content-muted mb-3">
                @addedNote
                @if (addedNoteHref is not null)
                {
                    <a class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-content" href="@addedNoteHref">Open that project's To-do tab</a>
                }
            </p>
        }

        <LoadGate IsLoading="@loading" Prominent="true" Label="Loading to-dos">
        @if (listFailed)
        {
            <p class="text-sm text-negative">Couldn't load the to-do list. The reason is in the red bar above — reload to try again.</p>
        }
        else
        {
            var visible = boardView ? FilteredItems : VisibleItems;
            <div class="panel p-5">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-1">
                    <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold text-content">@(boardView ? "To-do board" : "To-do list")</h2>
                    @if (items.Count > 0)
                    {
                        <span class="text-[11px] text-content-subtle">@items.Count(i => i.IsComplete) of @items.Count done</span>
                    }
                </div>
                <p class="text-xs text-content-subtle mb-4">
                    @(boardView
                        ? "Click a card for its full detail, linked to-dos and communications — or drag it between the columns to mark it done or reopen it."
                        : "Click an item for its full detail, linked to-dos and communications.")
                </p>

                @if (visible.Count == 0)
                {
                    <p class="text-sm text-content-subtle italic">
                        @(items.Count == 0
                            ? (CanSeeAll ? "No to-do items yet." : "Nothing is assigned to your role right now.")
                            : HasQuery ? $"No to-dos match \"{search}\"."
                            : "Nothing matches these filters.")
                    </p>
                }
                else if (boardView)
                {
                    @* Cards a reader may not tick off (someone else's item on a CanSeeAll list is
                       never that here — the drag gate mirrors the API's tick-off rule) stay
                       clickable but not draggable. *@
                    <TodoBoard Items="@visible" Busy="@busy"
                               OnOpen="OpenItem"
                               OnSetComplete="args => SetComplete(args.Item, args.IsComplete)"
                               CanDrag="item => CanSeeAll || IsMine(item)"
                               Scope="ScopeChipFor" />
                }
                else
                {
                    <div class="space-y-2">
                        @foreach (var item in visible)
                        {
                            <button type="button" @key="item.TodoItemId" title="Open this item"
                                    class="w-full rounded-lg border border-line px-3 py-2 text-left hover:border-line-strong hover:bg-surface-raised"
                                    @onclick="() => OpenItem(item)">
                                <p class="truncate text-sm @(item.IsComplete ? "text-content-subtle line-through" : "text-content")">
                                    <span class="font-mono text-xs text-content-muted mr-2">@item.Reference</span>@item.Title
                                </p>
                                <div class="mt-1 flex items-center gap-x-3 overflow-hidden text-[11px] text-content-subtle">
                                    <span class="whitespace-nowrap @(IsGeneral(item) ? "text-accent font-medium" : "")">@ScopeLabel(item)</span>
                                    @if (item.IsInProgress)
                                    {
                                        <span class="inline-flex items-center rounded border border-amber-400/30 bg-amber-400/10 px-1.5 text-[10px] font-medium leading-4 whitespace-nowrap text-amber-400" title="Started — still open until it's marked done">In progress</span>
                                    }
                                    @if (item.DueAt is not null)
                                    {
                                        <span class="whitespace-nowrap @(IsOverdue(item) ? "text-negative font-medium" : "")">Due @item.DueAt.Value.LocalDateTime.ToString("d MMM yyyy")</span>
                                    }
                                    <TodoAssigneeBadge Item="item" />
                                    @if (item.CompletedAt is not null)
                                    {
                                        <span class="whitespace-nowrap">Done @item.CompletedAt.Value.LocalDateTime.ToString("d MMM yyyy")</span>
                                    }
                                    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(item.CreatedByEmail))
                                    {
                                        <span class="truncate">Added by @item.CreatedByEmail</span>
                                    }
                                </div>
                            </button>
                        }
                    </div>
                }

                @* A match standing outside the scope/assignee filters is invisible above, which
                   reads as "not found" — the one wrong answer a search must never give. Said
                   rather than silently overriding the filters: they were picked on purpose. *@
                @if (HiddenMatchCount > 0)
                {
                    <p class="text-[11px] text-content-subtle mt-2">
                        @HiddenMatchCount matching to-do@(HiddenMatchCount == 1 ? " is" : "s are") hidden by the project/assignee filters.
                    </p>
                }

                @* The same keywords searched against the projects mailbox — renders only for the
                   triage roles (the gate lives in the component). *@
                @if (HasQuery)
                {
                    <TaggedEmailSearch Query="@search" />
                }
            </div>
        }
        </LoadGate>

    </section>
}

<Modal IsOpen="@addOpen" Title="Add a to-do" ConfirmLabel="Add to-do item" CancelLabel="Cancel"
       OnConfirm="Add" OnCancel="CloseAdd">
    <div class="space-y-3">
        @if (addError is not null)
        {
            <Notice Tone="Tone.Negative">@addError</Notice>
        }

        @* The first question, because it decides what the item IS. Defaulted from the scope
           filter. A control, so it's disabled with its own placeholder while the project list
           lands rather than gated behind a spinner. SearchSelect's own blank row IS the
           company-wide/"pick one" entry — nothing is prepended to the options. *@
        <div>
            <label class="block text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-content-subtle font-semibold mb-1">Project</label>
            <SearchSelect Options="@ProjectOptions" Value="@newProject" ValueChanged="v => newProject = v"
                          Placeholder="@ProjectPickerPlaceholder" WidthClass="w-full"
                          Disabled="@(!filtersReady || projectsFailed)" />
        </div>

        <p class="text-xs text-content-subtle">
            @(newProject != ""
                ? $"This item goes on {NewProjectLabel}'s To-do tab, and on the dashboard of everyone holding the assigned role (or just the person it's assigned to by name)."
                : CanSeeAll
                    ? "A general item belongs to no project — it lives on this page and on the dashboard of everyone holding the assigned role (or just the person it's assigned to by name)."
                    : "Pick the project this item belongs to — only the managing director raises company-wide items.")
        </p>

        <FormField Label="Title" Value="@newTitle" OnChange="v => newTitle = v" Required="true" />

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
            <div>
                <label class="block text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-content-subtle font-semibold mb-1">Assigned to</label>
                <TodoAssigneeSelect Options="@assigneeOptions" Value="@newAssignee" ValueChanged="v => newAssignee = v" />
            </div>
            <FormField Label="Due" Value="@newDue" OnChange="v => newDue = v" InputType="date" />
        </div>

        <div>
            <label class="block text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-content-subtle font-semibold mb-1">Notes</label>
            <textarea class="field" rows="2" value="@newNotes" @oninput="OnNewNotesInput"></textarea>
        </div>
    </div>
</Modal>

